For a row flex container’s intrinsic width, Ladybird follows the flexbox
spec’s “web-compatible” algorithm: It sums the items’ min-content or
max-content contributions, except that an item that can’t move toward
its contribution, and whose flex basis is definite, contributes its
hypothetical main size. That’s what Blink’s FlexLayoutAlgorithm::
ComputeMinMaxSizeOfRowContainer() does, with its cant_move and
is_used_flex_basis_indefinite checks. Gecko’s nsFlexContainerFrame::
ComputeIntrinsicISize() sums its items’ min or preferred inline sizes,
with no exception. And Chrome passes the same subtests of row-005,
row-008 and row-use-cases-001 that Ladybird now does.
